---
title: Audit log
description: The append-only record of everything that happened.
section: Compliance
---

**Compliance → Audit Log** is the tenant's record of every consequential action, by administrators
and by users. It is **append-only**: entries cannot be edited or deleted from the dashboard, which
is what makes it worth anything as evidence.

## What gets recorded

Roughly sixty action types, grouped by prefix:

| Prefix | Covers |
| --- | --- |
| `admin.*` | Administrator sign-in and configuration changes. |
| `user.*` | User creation, updates, deletion, sign-in. |
| `token.*` | Token issue, refresh, revoke — including `token.reuse_detected`. |
| `role.*`, `group.*` | Assignments and removals. |
| `oauth_client.*` | Client created, updated, secret rotated. |
| `consent.*` | Consent granted or revoked. |
| `security.*` | IP blocks, risk decisions. |
| `external_provider.*` | Social login configuration. |
| `policy.accepted` | A user accepted a version of your terms or privacy policy. |
| `data.purged` | Retention purge ran. |

## The entries worth alerting on

Most of the log is routine. These are not:

- **`token.reuse_detected`** — a refresh token was presented twice. That should be impossible in
  normal operation, and GateKeeper's response is to revoke the whole token family. It means a
  refresh token leaked, and it is the single highest-signal entry in the log.
- **`oauth_client.secret_rotated`** — if you did not do this, someone else did.
- **`admin.*`** from an unfamiliar address — administrators can reconfigure your identity provider.

## Using it in an investigation

The usual shape of the question is "what happened to this account?", and the honest answer is that
the audit log alone rarely tells you. Pair it with **[Login Events](/docs/risk-policy)**: the audit
log tells you *what was done*, while login events tell you *how risky the sign-in that did it
looked*. A `user.password_changed` is unremarkable on its own and rather more interesting when the
login that preceded it scored 70 from a new country.

> [!NOTE]
> The audit log is scoped to a tenant. Actions against the organization itself, including admin
> account changes, are not in a tenant's log.
